INT. COMMUNITY THEATER - LOBBY - DAY

A sign: "OPENING NIGHT: HAMLET (THE MUSICAL)." Balloons sag. Paper-cup punch.

DOROTHEA "DOTTIE" PRYCE, 60s, the director, strides in with a bullhorn. Her stage manager, EZEKIEL "ZEKE" ADEYEMI, 30s, trails with a clipboard. The leading man, FINNEGAN WALSH, 40s, in a doublet and sneakers, paces. Their costume designer, LUPE HERRERA, 50s, carries a rack of capes. An usher, SAMMY ODUYA, 17, hands out programs.

DOTTIE
Places!

ZEKE
It's four hours to curtain, Dottie.

DOTTIE
Places is a state of mind.

FINNEGAN
I can't find my skull.

LUPE
Which skull?

FINNEGAN
Yorick.

LUPE
It's in the prop closet.

FINNEGAN
The closet is locked.

ZEKE
Sammy has the key.

Sammy pats his pockets. His face falls.

SAMMY
I put it somewhere safe.

DOTTIE
Where is safe?

SAMMY
That's the thing.

A ruckus at the door. A woman strides in carrying an enormous, glittering costume: ANTONIA "TONI" VASQUEZ-LIND, 50s, the producer's wife, in sequins and fury. She dumps the costume on the floor.

TONI
Who approved this?

LUPE
The director.

DOTTIE
I approved a cape.

TONI
It's a disco ball.

FINNEGAN
Ophelia's mad scene needs sparkle.

TONI
Ophelia is drowning, Finnegan.

FINNEGAN
In sequins.

Dottie lifts the bullhorn and blasts a siren. Everyone covers their ears.

DOTTIE
Enough! We have a show!

Silence. Sammy slowly raises the key from inside his sock.
